Write next week's gym workout from the current plan and this check-in. The daily tracker reads the text, so the shape below is required.

CLIENT DETAILS:
[CLIENT DETAILS]

HOW TO WRITE THE UPDATE:

1. Open with 2 to 4 sentences to the client by first name. Use one fact from the check-in: sessions completed, energy, sleep, pain, or a request. Say what stays and what changes. Do not mention a coaching week number. NEVER write "Welcome to week", "for next week", or a cross-day pointer.

2. Keep the split already on file unless this check-in asked for a different one. If they asked for push/pull/legs or upper/lower, use that. If the current week is full body, replace it with upper/lower or push/pull/legs. Do not invent a new split because a month ended. Training days per week is a hard cap. Leftover days are Rest.

3. Label every day "Day N (Weekday)" with Day 1 = Monday through Day 7 = Sunday. After the colon, use a plain focus: Push, Pull, Legs, Upper, Lower, Chest and arms, Back and arms, or Rest. Example: "Day 2 (Tuesday): Pull". Do not put Power, Hypertrophy, or Density in the day title.
   A Push or Chest day is presses, dips, raises, and triceps only. No row, pulldown, or pull-up.
   A Pull or Back day is rows, pulldowns, pull-ups, and biceps curls only. No bench press, incline press, shoulder press, dip, push-up, or fly.
   An Upper day may include a press and a row. No squat, leg press, lunge, hinge, or calf raise.
   A Lower or Legs day is a squat or leg press plus a Romanian deadlift. No press, row, pulldown, curl, or triceps movement.
   Do not put the same working exercise on two calendar days in a row.

4. Use only common exercises a person can search on YouTube: Barbell Bench Press, Dumbbell Bench Press, Incline Bench Press, Shoulder Press, Push Up, Dip, Lat Pulldown, Seated Row, Dumbbell Row, Barbell Row, Pull Up, Squat, Leg Press, Romanian Deadlift, Deadlift, Lunge, Split Squat, Leg Curl, Leg Extension, Hip Thrust, Calf Raise, Biceps Curl, Triceps Pushdown, Lateral Raise, Face Pull, Plank, Crunch. Do not invent a move. Do not write Pallof press, prone Y raise, tempo, pause, or deficit in the name. Swap a blocked lift for another name on this list.

5. Under every training day: Warm-up: then Main Workout: then Post-Workout:. Warm-up is an easy walk or bike and joint circles only. Never repeat a Main Workout exercise in the warm-up. Each exercise name once per day. Dumbbell Bench Press and Barbell Bench Press are the same bench press, so write only one. Write only one row. About 5 to 7 working exercises. Post-Workout is stretches only. Do not add a shared warmup after the week. The daily tracker cannot resolve cross-day references. NEVER write "same as Day 1" or "repeat Monday". Rewrite the complete exercise list under every day.

6. Write "Exercise: N sets x M reps. One short sentence in everyday words." Example: "Lat Pulldown: 4 sets x 10 reps. Pull the bar toward your upper chest, then let your arms lengthen." Use a plain letter x. Never 5 or more working sets. Strong adherence: add a little load or a rep on the main lifts, not extra sets. Poor sleep or low energy: easier weights, same sets, and "stop when you could still do 2 more clean reps." Pain: swap the painful lift immediately. Do not write RIR, RDL, hypertrophy, mesocycle, or delts.

7. Use only their gym stations. One daily step line and a short sleep note. No Cardio or Supplements header. No calorie targets. Close with two sentences. Never use a hyphen, en dash, or em dash.

8. If anything here conflicts with Hard Constraints, obey Hard Constraints.
